/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   ClearSky-OMEGA · STORAGE rules addition — verification documents

   ⚠ A FRAGMENT. THIS IS NOT YOUR STORAGE RULES FILE.

   Firestore rules and Storage rules are two separate files with two separate
   deploy targets. I have your firestore.rules; I have NOT seen your
   storage.rules, and it almost certainly contains the buckets the editor, the
   intake form and the financing portal already write to.

   So: paste the ONE match block below into your existing storage.rules,
   inside the existing `match /b/{bucket}/o { ... }`. Do not replace the file.
   Replacing it would silently revoke every upload path the platform already
   depends on, and the failure would show up as "attachments stopped working"
   somewhere unrelated a week later.

   If the helper functions below already exist in your file under the same
   names, delete the duplicates here and keep yours — same reasoning as the
   Firestore fragment.

   WITHOUT THIS DEPLOYED: a reviewer works a whole assessment, presses upload
   on the bankability PDF, and it fails at the last step. Everything else in
   the verification console works fine, which makes it look like a file
   problem rather than a rules problem.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */


/* ── PASTE FROM HERE, INSIDE match /b/{bucket}/o ──────────────────────────── */

    function vSignedIn() {
      return request.auth != null && request.auth.token.email != null;
    }
    function vCallerDomain() {
      return request.auth.token.email.lower().split('@')[1];
    }
    function vIsStaff() {
      return vSignedIn()
        && vCallerDomain() in ['clearsky-usa.com', 'csebuilders.com'];
    }

    /* Path is verifications/{verificationId}/{file}, keyed by verification id
       first, so the partner check is a plain string comparison on the PATH.

       WHY NOT A FIRESTORE LOOKUP. Storage rules can read Firestore, but every
       such read costs a document get on every file operation — and the rule
       gets deployed without it the first time somebody is in a hurry. Keying
       the path so a comparison suffices removes the temptation.

       THE TRADE, STATED PLAINLY: any signed-in user can write into any {vid}
       folder, because the path alone does not say whose assignment it is.
       That is acceptable here because the Firestore document is what makes a
       file visible — an upload nobody can attach to a record nobody can find
       is litter, not a breach. If that ever stops holding (public listing, a
       bucket-wide index, anything that makes the path itself enumerable),
       move to the Firestore lookup and pay for it.

       Delete is staff-only. Once a verdict is signed its documents are part
       of the record, and the Firestore rules refuse to remove them from the
       document — a partner who could still delete the underlying file would
       leave the record pointing at nothing. */
    /* ── Deal documents ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Uploaded PDFs on a portfolio deal: signed reports, stamped drawings,
       executed agreements.

       WHAT BELONGS HERE vs IN DRIVE. Uploads are for FINAL artifacts — the
       version somebody signed, stamped or executed, which will never be
       edited again. Working files stay in Drive and are linked instead,
       because a copy of a living document goes stale the moment somebody
       edits the original and nobody can tell which is current. That is the
       whole reason the Files tab holds both kinds side by side.

       THE ACCESS TRADE, stated plainly, because deal documents can include
       financial models. Read is `signed in`, checked on the PATH rather than
       by looking the deal up in Firestore — a Firestore read on every file
       operation is a cost the rule gets deployed without the first time
       somebody is in a hurry. In practice exposure is narrow: the deal id is
       a random Firestore id, and Firebase download URLs carry an unguessable
       token, so a file is not reachable without someone handing over the
       link. But it is NOT the same guarantee as the Firestore rules give the
       deal record itself.

       So: if a document would be damaging in the hands of any approved portal
       user, link it from Drive instead, where the permission is real. Say so
       to whoever is uploading — the console does. */
    match /deals/{dealId}/{file} {
      allow read:   if vSignedIn();
      allow write:  if vSignedIn()
                    && request.resource.size < 50 * 1024 * 1024
                    && request.resource.contentType.matches(
                         'application/pdf|image/.*');
      /* Staff only, and the Firestore record keeps its entry either way — a
         missing file is visible as a broken link rather than silently gone. */
      allow delete: if vIsStaff();
    }

    match /verifications/{vid}/{file} {
      allow read:   if vSignedIn();
      allow write:  if vSignedIn()
                    && request.resource.size < 50 * 1024 * 1024
                    && request.resource.contentType.matches(
                         'application/pdf|image/.*|application/vnd.*|text/.*');
      allow delete: if vIsStaff();
    }

/* ── PASTE TO HERE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */


/* If you have no storage.rules at all yet, the complete minimal file is:

rules_version = '2';
service firebase.storage {
  match /b/{bucket}/o {

    // ... your existing blocks would go here ...

    // the block above
  }
}

   But check first — an existing bucket almost always has rules already, and
   "I didn't find the file" is not the same as "there isn't one".
*/
